WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.560
How does depth-first search work?

2
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.260
Follow one branch as far

3
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.240
as possible, then backtrack to

4
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.560
the last vertex with an

5
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.800
unexplored neighbour. The call stack

6
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.100
remembers the active path, so

7
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:12.900
you need not.

8
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.280
Use the same directed graph:

9
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.340
A reaches B and C,

10
00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.660
each can reach D, and

11
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.540
D reaches E. Choose neighbours

12
00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.980
alphabetically and mark a vertex

13
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.760
immediately when its call begins.

14
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.900
From A, discover B and

15
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:30.960
leave A waiting on the

16
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.080
stack. From B, discover D,

17
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.160
then from D discover E;

18
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.760
each recursive call suspends the

19
00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:38.560
previous one.

20
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.355
E has no outgoing neighbour

21
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.260
to explore, so finish E

22
00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.040
and return to D. D

23
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.080
is then finished, followed by

24
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.960
B, and the stack returns

25
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.540
to the suspended call for

26
00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:50.840
A.

27
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.706
A still has its neighbour

28
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.380
C, so discover C now.

29
00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.540
C points to D, but

30
00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.180
D is already visited and

31
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.600
finished; skip it, finish C,

32
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.080
then finish A.

33
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.820
The discovery order was A,

34
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.820
B, D, E, C. The

35
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.200
finishing order was E, D,

36
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.840
B, C, A; writing the

37
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.540
discovery order when asked for

38
00:01:15.562 --> 00:01:17.820
postorder would produce a completely

39
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:18.800
different answer.

40
00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.720
A search from one source

41
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.980
only visits reachable vertices. For

42
00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.840
the whole graph, loop over

43
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.560
every vertex and start another

44
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.340
call at each unvisited one;

45
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.160
the result is a DFS

46
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:30.620
forest.

47
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.200
For directed cycle detection, distinguish

48
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.000
undiscovered, active, and finished vertices.

49
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:38.740
An edge to an active

50
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.500
ancestor gives a back edge

51
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.180
and a cycle; an edge

52
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.700
to an already finished vertex

53
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:44.800
alone does not.

54
00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.160
Each vertex is first discovered

55
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.360
once and each adjacency entry

56
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.560
is examined once. Initialisation contributes

57
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.500
order n and edge scanning

58
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.560
contributes order m, so the

59
00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.180
full forest takes order n

60
00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.120
plus m with adjacency lists.

61
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.820
Visited and parent arrays use

62
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.560
order n space, and a

63
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.079
long chain can create n

64
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.460
active recursive calls. An explicit

65
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.440
stack avoids a language recursion

66
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.720
limit, but reproducing the exact

67
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.060
recursive order requires tracking where

68
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.200
each call resumes.

69
00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.700
Things to take note of.

70
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.020
DFS does not generally find

71
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.200
shortest paths, and neighbour order

72
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.620
can change its tree and

73
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.420
orders. In undirected simple graphs,

74
00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.220
scanning the reverse parent edge

75
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.540
is not evidence of a

76
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:30.860
cycle.

77
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.560
With parallel edges, track the

78
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.580
parent edge identity rather than

79
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.060
skipping every edge to that

80
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.820
vertex. Discover on entry, finish

81
00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.800
on return, and backtrack only

82
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.400
after a branch is exhausted.

83
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.820
There, wandering with a proper

84
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:45.220
memory.
